= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A <group representation> on a <vector space> $V$ is a <group homomorphism> $R:G\to\operatorname{GL}(V)$: $R(gh)=R(g)R(h)$ and $R(e)=I$. A <reducible representation> has a nonzero proper <invariant subspace> $W\subset V$, meaning $R(g)W\subseteq W$ for every $g$. Over the <complex numbers>, a finite-dimensional <representation> of a <finite group> is a <direct sum> of <irreducible representations>, by <Maschke's theorem>.

The <character of a representation> is $\chi_R(g)=\operatorname{tr}R(g)$. The cyclic property of the <trace> makes it a <class function>:
$$
\chi_R(hgh^{-1})=\operatorname{tr}\bigl(R(h)R(g)R(h)^{-1}\bigr)=\chi_R(g).
$$
Thus a <character> is specified by one number per <conjugacy class>, much less information than all the <representation> <matrices>. Moreover,
$$
\chi_{R\oplus S}=\chi_R+\chi_S,\qquad
\chi_{R\otimes S}=\chi_R\chi_S,\qquad
\boxed{m_r=\frac1{|G|}\sum_{g\in G}\overline{\chi_r(g)}\chi_R(g).}
$$
Here <character orthogonality> says that $m_r$ is the multiplicity of the <irreducible representation> $r$. Consequently <characters> determine finite-dimensional complex <representations> up to <equivalence of group representations> and compute <tensor product of group representations> decompositions without constructing a new <basis> explicitly.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Compose <permutations> from right to left. The three <transpositions> are $\tau_1=(23)$, $\tau_2=(13)$ and $\tau_3=(12)$. In <cycle notation>, direct composition gives
$$
\tau_1\tau_2=(123),\qquad \tau_2\tau_1=(132),\qquad \tau_i^2=e.
$$
The complete <symmetric group> is therefore
$$
\boxed{S_3=\{e,\tau_1,\tau_2,\tau_3,\tau_1\tau_2,\tau_2\tau_1\}.}
$$
<Conjugation> relabels the entries of a <cycle>, so the <conjugacy classes> are precisely
$$
\boxed{\{e\},\qquad\{\tau_1,\tau_2,\tau_3\},\qquad\{\tau_1\tau_2,\tau_2\tau_1\}.}
$$
Their sizes are $1,3,2$, respectively. The differing <orders of group elements> also show that these three sets cannot merge under <conjugation>.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

There is one complex <irreducible representation> for each <conjugacy class>, hence three. The <trivial representation> $\mathbf1$ and <sign representation> $\varepsilon$ are distinct and one-dimensional. The <sum of squares of irreducible representation dimensions> leaves
$$
1^2+1^2+d^2=6,\qquad\boxed{d=2.}
$$
Call the remaining <irreducible representation> $\mathbf2$. The <permutation representation> on $\mathbb C^3$ has <character> equal to the number of fixed objects, namely $(3,1,0)$ on the ordered <conjugacy classes> $(e,\text{transpositions},\text{three-cycles})$. Removing its constant-vector <trivial representation> gives $(2,0,-1)$, whose <character inner product> with itself is one. This constructs $\mathbf2$ and gives the <character table>
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|rrr}
\text{class}&e&(12)&(123)\\
\text{class size}&1&3&2\\\hline
\chi_{\mathbf1}&1&1&1\\
\chi_{\varepsilon}&1&-1&1\\
\chi_{\mathbf2}&2&0&-1
\end{array}.}
$$
To obtain every <Clebsch-Gordan decomposition of group representations>, multiply the <characters> pointwise. In particular, $\chi_\varepsilon^2=\chi_{\mathbf1}$, $\chi_\varepsilon\chi_{\mathbf2}=\chi_{\mathbf2}$ and
$$
\chi_{\mathbf2}^2=(4,0,1)=\chi_{\mathbf1}+\chi_\varepsilon+\chi_{\mathbf2}.
$$
Thus all ordered pairs of <irreducible representations> are covered by
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|ccc}
\otimes&\mathbf1&\varepsilon&\mathbf2\\\hline
\mathbf1&\mathbf1&\varepsilon&\mathbf2\\
\varepsilon&\varepsilon&\mathbf1&\mathbf2\\
\mathbf2&\mathbf2&\mathbf2&\mathbf1\oplus\varepsilon\oplus\mathbf2
\end{array}.}
$$

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

All three displayed <transposition> <matrices> have <trace> zero. Multiplying two of them gives
$$
R(\tau_1\tau_2)=\operatorname{diag}(\alpha,\alpha^{-1}),\qquad
R(\tau_2\tau_1)=\operatorname{diag}(\alpha^{-1},\alpha),
$$
since $\alpha^3=1$. Their <traces> are $\alpha+\alpha^{-1}=-1$, whereas $\operatorname{tr}R(e)=2$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\chi_R=(2,0,-1),\qquad
\langle\chi_R,\chi_R\rangle=\frac{4+3\cdot0+2\cdot1}{6}=1.}
$$
By <character orthogonality>, $R$ is the <irreducible representation> $\mathbf2$ from <1/c>.

There is also a direct <invariant subspace> argument. The two distinct <eigenvalues> of $R(\tau_1\tau_2)$ force any invariant line to be one of the two coordinate lines. But $R(\tau_3)$ exchanges those lines. Neither is invariant under the whole <group>, so this two-dimensional <representation> is irreducible.

= e
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The <scalar fields> commute, so a degree-$d$ expression in one doublet belongs to a <symmetric power>, rather than an arbitrary <tensor product>. Put $x=\phi_1$, $y=\phi_2$. The <generators of a group> $r=\tau_1\tau_2$ and $s=\tau_3$ act by
$$
r:(x,y)\mapsto(\alpha x,\alpha^{-1}y),\qquad s:(x,y)\mapsto(y,x).
$$
A <monomial> $x^a y^b$ is fixed by $r$ precisely when $a-b$ is divisible by three. Imposing $s$ then pairs the exponents. Through degree four the resulting holomorphic <polynomial invariants> are
$$
\begin{array}{c|l}
\text{degree}&\text{basis}\\\hline
0&1\\
1&\text{none}\\
2&xy\\
3&x^3+y^3\\
4&x^2y^2
\end{array}
$$
In particular, two explicit invariant terms are $\phi_1\phi_2$ and $\phi_1^3+\phi_2^3$. Their invariance follows directly from $\alpha^3=1$ and the interchange of the two components. A quartic example is $(\phi_1\phi_2)^2$.

For a physical real <scalar potential>, <complex conjugate> fields are allowed as well. The <Hermitian inner product> $\phi^\dagger\phi$ and its square are also invariant, while a holomorphic term can enter with its <complex conjugate>, for example $b(\phi_1^3+\phi_2^3)+\bar b(\bar\phi_1^3+\bar\phi_2^3)$. There is still no invariant linear term, because neither the doublet nor its <conjugate representation> contains a <trivial representation>. Thus the possible nonconstant total degrees are
$$
\boxed{S_3:\quad 2,3,4.}
$$
The constant term is unrestricted.

For the <fundamental representation> of the <special unitary group> $SU(2)$, its central element $-I$ sends $\phi\mapsto-\phi$ and $\bar\phi\mapsto-\bar\phi$. Every odd-degree <polynomial> changes sign, excluding degrees one and three. Further, $SU(2)$ acts transitively on the unit sphere in $\mathbb C^2$: a unit vector $(u,v)^T$ is the first column of
$$
\begin{pmatrix}u&-\bar v\\v&\bar u\end{pmatrix}\in SU(2).
$$
An invariant <polynomial> must therefore depend only on $\phi^\dagger\phi$. Through quartic order the general real <scalar potential> is
$$
\boxed{V=V_0+m^2\phi^\dagger\phi+\lambda(\phi^\dagger\phi)^2,
\qquad V_0,m^2,\lambda\in\mathbb R.}
$$
Hence the possible nonconstant degrees are $2,4$; the cubic term permitted by $S_3$ is excluded by $SU(2)$.

If the notation $V(\phi)$ is instead read as restricting to holomorphic <polynomials> alone, the distinction matters: a single commuting $SU(2)$ doublet has no nonconstant holomorphic <polynomial invariant>. The candidate contraction $\epsilon_{ab}\phi_a\phi_b=\phi_1\phi_2-\phi_2\phi_1$ vanishes identically. The quadratic and quartic $SU(2)$ terms above require <complex conjugate> fields. This also explains why counting a <trivial representation> in $\mathbf2\otimes\mathbf2$ alone would give an incorrect holomorphic answer.

